1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,300
Section 4

2
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:10,626
Today, we'll be continuing the series of lectures on memory by focusing on what is called episodic memory

3
00:00:10,626 --> 00:00:15,500
and what can happen if this is not working properly.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:21,537
Episodic memory refers to the memory of an event or 'episode'.

5
00:00:21,537 --> 00:00:29,019
Episodic memories allow us to mentally travel back in time to an event from

6
00:00:29,019 --> 00:00:30,100
the past.

7
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:36,862
Episodic memories include various details about these events, for example,

8
00:00:36,862 --> 00:00:44,300
when an event happened and other information such as the location.

9
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:51,053
To help understand this concept, try to remember the last time you ate dinner

10
00:00:51,053 --> 00:00:52,500
at a restaurant.

11
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,939
The ability to remember where you ate,

12
00:00:55,939 --> 00:01:02,817
who you were with and the items you ordered are all features of an

13
00:01:02,817 --> 00:01:03,800
episodic memory.

14
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:12,000
Episodic memory is distinct from another type of memory called semantic memory.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,122
This is the type of factual memory that we have in common with everyone

16
00:01:19,122 --> 00:01:23,700
else - that is your general knowledge of the world.

17
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:31,594
To build upon a previous example, remembering where you parked your car is an

18
00:01:31,594 --> 00:01:33,850
example of episodic memory,

19
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:41,744
but your understanding of what a car is and how an engine works are

20
00:01:41,744 --> 00:01:44,000
examples of semantic memory.

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Unlike episodic memory, semantic memory isn't dependent on recalling personal experiences.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:04,500
Episodic memory can be thought of as a process with several different steps of

23
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,000
memory processing:

24
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
encoding, consolidation and retrieval.

25
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,857
The initial step is called encoding.

26
00:02:12,857 --> 00:02:18,643
This involves the process of receiving and registering information,

27
00:02:18,643 --> 00:02:27,000
which is necessary for creating memories of information or events that you experience.

28
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,525
The degree to which you can successfully encode information depends on the level of

29
00:02:34,525 --> 00:02:39,900
attention you give to an event while it's actually happening.

30
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:46,400
Being distracted can make effective encoding very difficult.

31
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:55,000
Encoding of episodic memories is also influenced by how you process the event.

32
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,382
For example, if you were introduced to someone called Charlie, you might make the

33
00:03:02,382 --> 00:03:06,600
connection that your uncle has the same name.

34
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:13,911
Future recollection of Charlie's name is much easier if you have a strategy to

35
00:03:13,911 --> 00:03:16,000
help you encode it.

36
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:25,200
Memory consolidation, the next step in forming an episodic memory, is the process by

37
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:34,400
which memories of encoded information are strengthened, stabilised and stored to facilitate later retrieval.

38
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:44,122
Consolidation is most effective when the information being stored can be linked to an

39
00:03:44,122 --> 00:03:46,900
existing network of information.

40
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:55,100
Consolidation makes it possible for you to store memories for later retrieval indefinitely.

41
00:03:55,100 --> 00:04:04,100
Forming strong memories depends on the frequency with which you try to retrieve them.

42
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:11,800
Memories can fade or become harder to retrieve if they aren't used very often.

43
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:18,522
The last step in forming episodic memories is called retrieval,

44
00:04:18,522 --> 00:04:23,900
which is the conscious recollection of encoded information.

45
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:36,600
Retrieving information from episodic memory depends upon semantic, olfactory, auditory and visual factors.

46
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:43,600
These help episodic memory retrieval by acting as a prompt.

47
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:44,627
For example,

48
00:04:44,627 --> 00:04:51,818
when recalling where you parked your car you may use the colour of a

49
00:04:51,818 --> 00:04:54,900
sign close to where you parked.

50
00:04:54,900 --> 00:05:01,300
You actually have to mentally travel back to the moment you parked.

51
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:10,540
There are a wide range of neurological diseases and conditions that can affect episodic

52
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:11,200
memory.

53
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:18,200
These range from Alzheimer's to schizophrenia to autism.

54
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:27,300
An impairment of episodic memory can have a profound effect on individuals' lives.

55
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:37,100
For example, the symptoms of schizophrenia can be reasonably well controlled by medication;

56
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:47,990
however, patients' episodic memory may still be impaired and so they are often unable to return to university

57
00:05:47,990 --> 00:05:49,200
or work.

58
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:57,968
Recent studies have shown that computer-assisted games designed to keep the brain active can

59
00:05:57,968 --> 00:06:01,100
help improve their episodic memory.

60
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:10,676
Episodic memories can help people connect with others, for instance by sharing intimate details

61
00:06:10,676 --> 00:06:12,728
about their past:

62
00:06:12,728 --> 00:06:18,200
something individuals with autism often have problems with.

63
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:24,600
This may be caused by an absence of a sense of self.

64
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:29,460
This is essential for the storage of episodic memory,

65
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:35,400
and has been found to be impaired in children with autism.

66
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:44,422
Research has shown that treatments that improve memory may also have a positive impact

67
00:06:44,422 --> 00:06:47,000
on children's social development.

